  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069110/assets/4c1a8cde010abbeaf318a64e6c9f3b6cbd3e7bac84babbcda4c1ebfb483fa2b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $s=\\lambda m+\\mu n$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069110/assets/e018cc5b4760b578d9ca514f9de6641e823bf5f6a8e17f019ebf24fc6ccb2248.png)\n\nIn $\u25b3OTS$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OT=4|m|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ and $m$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-4m.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $TS=2|n|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{TS}$ and $n$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{TS}=2n.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =\\overset{\\to }{OT}+\\overset{\\to }{TS} \\\\  & =-4m+2n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $s=\\lambda m+\\mu n=-4m+2n.$\n\nSince $m\u2226n,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =2$",
